 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed changes.** They provide fast updates without a realtime provider's recurring fees or coupling, at the cost of operating connections and reconnect recovery. Five-second HTTPS polling is simpler and stateless but adds repeated reads and seconds of lag. A hosted realtime service reduces connection operations but introduces recurring fees and provider coupling. All three can meet the 15-second freshness goal. See [the delivery decision](decisions/change-delivery.md) for the tradeoffs and downstream implications.
